# CMake coding rules

- Use lowercase built-in command names and four-space indentation.
- Prefer target properties and `target_*` commands over directory-wide include paths, definitions, compile options, or
  link settings.
- Declare target sources, public headers, dependencies, and install components explicitly.
- Use existing project helper APIs instead of reproducing their package, install, or test logic.
- Keep reusable helper APIs independent of particular package, extension, and module names.
- Validate helper arguments and fail with an actionable `message(FATAL_ERROR ...)` for contract violations.
- Quote paths and values that may contain spaces. Leave list expansions unquoted only when list semantics are intended.
- Use generator expressions for configuration-dependent target behavior.
- Resolve dependencies through imported targets instead of ambient include or library directories.
- Use `CONFIGURE_DEPENDS` only when a glob intentionally detects files for validation or staging; do not use source
  globs in place of an authored target inventory.
- Keep platform branches narrow and verify every supported platform affected by a change.
- Format long calls with one logical argument per line and close the call at the original indentation level.
